#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# rest

#% text
# `rest(xs)` gives the list `xs` without its first element.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `rest(xs)` is `drop(xs, 1)`; `most(xs)` drops the last element instead.

#% item
# The rest of a one-element list is `[]`; of an empty list, a failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
rest([a, b, c, d])
#% output
#= [b, c, d]

#% cell
rest([x])
#% output
#= []

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Any compound term keeps its head:

#% cell
rest(f(1, 2, 3))
#% output
#= f(2, 3)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Differences of neighbouring elements:

#% cell
xs = [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]
rest(xs) - most(xs)
#% output
#= [3, 5, 7, 9]

#% text
# The ratios of consecutive Fibonacci numbers, approaching the golden ratio:

#% cell
fib = map(first, nest_list(p => [p[2], p[1] + p[2]], [1, 1], 10))
n(rest(fib)/most(fib))
#% output
#= [1.0, 2.0, 1.5, 1.6666666666666667, 1.6, 1.625, 1.6153846153846154, 1.619047619047619, 1.6176470588235294, 1.6181818181818182]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `first` and `rest` together rebuild the list:

#% cell
prepend(rest(xs), first(xs)) === xs
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The same as `drop` with 1:

#% cell
rest(xs) === drop(xs, 1)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% cell
rest([])
#% output
#! failure(index, 1, 0)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `most`, `first`, `drop`, `take`, `last`
